# Writing XPath Queries ✍️

- `/` : Selects from the root node (Absolute path).
- `//`: Selects nodes from anywhere in the document (Relative path).
- `@` : Selects attributes.

## Examples
```xpath_not
/* Select all <div> elements anywhere in the document */
//div

/* Select the <a> tags that are direct children of <div> tags */
//div/a

/* Select the href attribute of all <a> tags */
//a/@href
```

# Filtering with Predicates 🛡️

Predicates are used to find a specific node or a node that contains a specific value. They are written in square brackets `[]`.

```xpath_not
/* Select the first <p> element */
//p[1]

/* Select the last <p> element */
//p[last()]

/* Select all <div> elements that have a class attribute */
//div[@class]

/* Select all <div> elements where the class is exactly "container" */
//div[@class="container"]
```

# Contains and Text Matching 🧠

XPath can do things CSS selectors can't!

## Contains
Useful when an element has multiple classes (e.g., `<div class="btn btn-primary large">`):
```xpath_not
//div[contains(@class, "btn-primary")]
```

## Text Matching
Find an element based on the text inside it!
```xpath_not
/* Exact match */
//button[text()="Submit"]

/* Partial match */
//a[contains(text(), "Click")]
```
